---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/molybdenum`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Molybdenum Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Molybdenum', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Molybdenum: Sulfite Oxidase, TPN Deficiency, and Why Isolated Supplementation Is Rare"
  description="Molybdenum reference — RDA, its role as sulfite oxidase and xanthine oxidase cofactor, TPN-induced deficiency (Abumrad case), food sources, and toxicity."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/molybdenum", label: "Molybdenum" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Molybdenum</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Molybdenum is a cofactor for four human enzymes: sulfite oxidase (metabolises
      sulphur amino acids), xanthine oxidase (catabolises purines to uric acid), aldehyde
      oxidase, and mitochondrial amidoxime-reducing component. Isolated dietary deficiency
      has essentially never been documented in humans on normal diets.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults:</strong> 45 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 50 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 50 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 2,000 µg/day.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Abumrad case — how we know molybdenum is essential</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      In 1981, Abumrad et al. reported a patient on long-term TPN who developed intolerance
      to sulphur amino acids, tachycardia, headache, night blindness, and coma. Molybdenum
      supplementation reversed everything within days. This is the case that established
      molybdenum as a human essential trace mineral. Real-world dietary deficiency in
      free-living adults has not been reported.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Molybdenum cofactor deficiency (genetic)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A rare autosomal recessive disorder causing loss of sulfite oxidase, xanthine oxidase,
      and aldehyde oxidase activity. Presents in the neonatal period with intractable
      seizures, developmental regression, and death in infancy unless treated with cyclic
      pyranopterin monophosphate (cPMP, fosdenopterin, approved 2021). Not a "dietary
      molybdenum" issue — the biosynthetic pathway is broken.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Toxicity</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      High dietary intake (10–15 mg/day, ~200–300× the RDA) has been reported to cause
      gout-like syndrome via xanthine oxidase induction and elevated uric acid.
      Environmental cases in high-molybdenum soil regions (parts of Armenia) documented
      this in the 1960s. Not a real-world concern from supplements or normal diets in
      most of the world.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Black-eyed peas (1 cup cooked): 288 µg — 6× the RDA in one serving</li>
      <li>Lima beans (1 cup cooked): 210 µg</li>
      <li>Lentils (1 cup cooked): 148 µg</li>
      <li>Soybeans (1 cup): 130 µg</li>
      <li>Milk (1 cup): 33 µg</li>
      <li>Yoghurt (1 cup): 26 µg</li>
      <li>Peanuts (1 oz): 12 µg</li>
      <li>Beef liver (3 oz): 40 µg</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      Any diet containing legumes covers molybdenum many times over. Isolated supplementation
      is essentially unnecessary in normal circumstances.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Neonatal seizures with unusual biochemistry
      warrant urgent metabolic workup — this is where the rare molybdenum-cofactor
      deficiency is diagnosed.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/molybdenum" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
